  "response": "Adjudicating between transmission and independent origin begins with the theoretical commitments that each side brings to the evidence. Diffusionism holds that cultural traits spread outward from a point of origin through contact. Franz Boas emphasized detailed regional historical reconstruction over broad evolutionary schemes. Set against that premise of spread from a single source, by contrast, stands the rival position that resemblance between distant societies need involve no contact whatever. Independent invention accounts explain cross-cultural similarities as separate responses to comparable conditions. Arbitrary non-functional details shared by two practices weigh against independent invention.\n\nThat programme of close regional reconstruction led directly to the distributional test that still carries most of the argumentative weight: the presence of the trait in intervening regions supports a diffusion route between two distant societies, whereas an account resting on separate responses to similar conditions predicts no such intervening corridor of occurrences. The age-area hypothesis infers that a trait with a wide distribution is older than one with a narrow distribution. Radiocarbon dating establishes the chronological order in which a practice appears in each region. Radiocarbon dating measures the decay of potassium-40 in organic tissue, and it is this measurement that shows the order of first appearances region by region to be recoverable at all.\n\nChronology alone, however, does not settle comparative inference, because the units entered into a comparison are rarely independent of one another. Galton's problem is the statistical non-independence of cultures sampled in cross-cultural comparisons, and recognition of that difficulty led to phylogenetic comparative methods, which control for shared ancestry when testing correlations across cultures. Properly specified, such models hold apart resemblance inherited from a common ancestral culture, resemblance acquired through borrowing, and resemblance generated afresh.\n\nThe Polynesian sweet potato is the case on which these considerations are most often exercised. Sweet potato remains excavated in the Cook Islands date to approximately 1000 CE. The sweet potato is native to Southeast Asia, and that provenance confirms that genomic analysis found no Native American ancestry in pre-contact Polynesian populations. That absence and the finding that genomic analysis identified Native American ancestry in Polynesian genomes dating to around 1200 CE cannot both hold, and exactly one of them must: either the pre-contact populations were genetically untouched by the Americas, or admixture was already present by the thirteenth century. The Cook Islands tubers, for their part, lay in the ground some two centuries before the genomes in which that ancestry appears.",
